    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [X] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [X] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n- [X] I have updated 'mini.nvim' to latest version\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.surround\n\n### Description\n\nThis is my config in it's entirety for mini.surround:\r\n\r\n```\r\n\t\t\trequire(\"mini.surround\").setup({\r\n\t\t\t\tmappings = {\r\n\t\t\t\t\tadd = \"<leader>ci\",\r\n\t\t\t\t\treplace = \"<leader>ca\",\r\n\t\t\t\t\tdelete = \"<leader>cd\",\r\n\t\t\t\t\tfind = \"<leader>cs\",\r\n\t\t\t\t\tfind_left = \"<leader>ch\",\r\n\t\t\t\t\thighlight = \"<leader>ct\",\r\n\t\t\t\t\tupdate_n_lines = \"<leader>cn\",\r\n\t\t\t\t\tsuffix_last = \"l\",\r\n\t\t\t\t\tsuffix_next = \"n\",\r\n\t\t\t\t},\r\n\t\t\t})\r\n\r\n\t\t\tvim.keymap.set(\"n\", \"<leader>c;\", \"<leader>ciaf(\", { desc = \"( wrap form )\" })\r\n\t\t\tvim.keymap.set(\"n\", \"<leader>c.\", \"<leader>ciae(\", { desc = \"( wrap element )\" })\r\n\t\t\tvim.keymap.set(\"n\", \"<leader>cx\", \"<leader>ciaf)\", { desc = \"(wrap form)\" })\r\n\t\t\tvim.keymap.set(\"n\", \"<leader>ck\", \"<leader>ciae)\", { desc = \"(wrap element)\" })\r\n\t\t\tvim.keymap.set(\"n\", \"<leader>cj\", \"<leader>ciaw)\", { desc = \"(wrap word)\" })\r\n\r\n```\r\n\r\nWhen entering a file, typing `<leader>ciaf(` works as expected. However all of the additional mapping (`<leader>c{;.xkj}`) just move the cursor one column to the right.\r\n\r\nI've tried all sorts of different mapping escapes and nothing seems to be trigger it.\r\n\r\nI have custom treesitter regions for e and f, but it fails all the same with vanilla motions like w\n\n### Neovim version\n\nv0.10.2\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n1. `nvim -nu minimal.lua`\r\n2. ...\r\n\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nI would expect `<leader>cx` to produce the same output of `<leader>ciaf)`\n\n### Actual behavior\n\n`<leader>ciaf)` will successfully wrap the form, while `<leader>cx` just moves the cursr to the right.",
